@group(:core)
mod Std.Char
  ## The Unicode code point — Cure's character type.
  ##
  ## A `Char` is a Unicode scalar: an index below the code-point ceiling
  ## `0x110000` (1114112). It is `Bounded(0x110000)`, so a `Char` value is one
  ## machine integer that erases to its code point. Character literals (`'a'`,
  ## `'\n'`) elaborate to `Char` values, and a `String` is a `List(Char)`. This
  ## module gives the type its visible, documented home, mirroring
  ## `Std.Int`/`Std.Float`/`Std.Binary`/`Std.Atom`.
  ##
  ## `use Std.Char` also brings `Bounded` into scope (via `use Std.Bounded`), so
  ## a bare character literal elaborates without a separate `Bounded` import.

  use Std.Bounded

  ## `@prelude`: `Char` is ambient like `String` (which is `List(Char)`), so a
  ## bare `Char` return/field type and character literals resolve with no `use`.
  @prelude
  typealias Char = Bounded(1114112)

  ## The Unicode code point of `c` as an `Int` (the Lean `Fin.val` analog for
  ## `Char = Bounded(0x110000)`). A `Char` already erases to its code point, so
  ## the runtime bridge is the identity; this names the `Char -> Int` coercion
  ## so `Std.Comparable`'s `Char`/`String` instances can compare code points. Named
  ## `code_point` (not `to_int`) because `Std.String` also exposes a `to_int`
  ## and the dependent pipeline resolves globals by bare name.
  @extern(:cure_std_char, :code_point, 1)
  fn code_point(c: Char) -> Int
